93
00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:33.719
lightclient: Yeah, one of the things that I've been looking into a little bit is if it's possible for the changes related to 8250 recent routes, if those could, you know, be treated as

94
00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:36.230
lightclient: You know, out of-protocol,

95
00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:43.709
lightclient: Like, basically out of-protocol rules in the transaction pool, the same way that we do with the expiry frame.

96
00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:54.290
lightclient: The main reason is… In my perspective, changing the protocol, changing the envelope of the transaction is… Always.

97
00:13:54.910 --> 00:14:10.700
lightclient: a much, you know, more difficult task, something that lives forever, it's something that we have to maintain, it's something that we can get very wrong and cause issues with consensus. So, if things can be done outside the protocol, that's almost always my preference.

98
00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:22.310
lightclient: And with Recent Roots, as we looked into it more during, like, our conversations for the tiering for Hegota, we just kept coming to this question, like, why does this have to be…

99
00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:42.519
lightclient: Oh, sorry, yeah, 8272. We just kept coming to this question, like, why does this need to be in a transaction envelope? It doesn't feel like there's anything that's particularly special about the recent routes. The special aspect of it is just that we're basically allowing the, you know, the users to read these routes.

100
00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.730
lightclient: In the public mempool.

101
00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:49.709
lightclient: And this, to me, is exactly what the canonical frames are for.

102
00:14:50.150 --> 00:15:05.310
lightclient: So I don't know if anybody has thought of this at all, or if they, you know, some… is there any initial pushback on this idea, or, like, reasons why it's not… it's not possible to, you know.

103
00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:10.300
lightclient: Keep this as an out-of-protocol assumption in the transaction pool.

104
00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:22.519
marc | wolovim: Chris, give you a thumbs up. Did you want to add to that, Chris?

105
00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:30.730
