108
00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:49.960
Toni Wahrstätter: Yeah, I think one of the initial motivations was definitely gas costs.

109
00:15:50.140 --> 00:15:57.230
Toni Wahrstätter: I'm not sure if, removing it from the transaction object would change something there.

110
00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:07.329
lightclient: Why… why is the gas cost so big of a concern?

111
00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:21.070
Toni Wahrstätter: Of course, it should be cheap. Like, the cheaper the better. Like, what you just proposed is you still have the root statically available in the transaction, right? It's just, like…

112
00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:29.070
Toni Wahrstätter: Or where would you move it? From the Transaction Object to?

113
00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:35.700
lightclient: It would basically be in a frame to the recent routes contract, very similar to how it's proposed.

114
00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:43.010
lightclient: It's just that instead of having the recent root list as an outer list on the transaction object, it's just the call data.

115
00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:45.470
lightclient: to the recent Roots contract.

116
00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:51.679
lightclient: And so you can statically reason about how the recent route Contract executes.

117
00:16:52.700 --> 00:16:58.710
lightclient: If you want. Or you can simply just execute it and determine if that, verify Frame passes or not.

118
00:16:59.160 --> 00:16:59.780
Toni Wahrstätter: Right.

119
00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:04.519
Toni Wahrstätter: Yeah, I think this is fine, as long as the root stays statically available.

120
00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:04.910
lightclient: Yeah.

121
00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:07.520
Toni Wahrstätter: Because otherwise, you could have, like, a reorg.

122
00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:19.279
Toni Wahrstätter: And if you don't have the root, you could… you wouldn't know if a transaction is actually, still, proving against the correct route on the right chain.

123
00:17:19.869 --> 00:17:24.150
Toni Wahrstätter: But since the root stays statically available, it should be fine.

124
00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:39.859
lightclient: Okay. Yeah, I mean, nothing necessarily changes about A141 for this, nothing necessarily changes about A272 for this.

125
00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:48.179
lightclient: Right now, like, we're still in the process of choosing the Eaps for Hegota. This is kind of my current perspective.

126
00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:57.040
lightclient: So, feel free to take some time offline and think more about it, and if there is a good reason that it should continue to exist in the envelope, then…

127
00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:00.900
lightclient: I'm happy to change my… my preference.

128
00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:02.910
lightclient: Tomorrow.

129
00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:14.029
soispoke: Hi, yeah, I'm sorry, I just joined, but, I think the validation guess I wanted to talk about,

130
00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:17.530
soispoke: Was that if you don't have it in the envelope.

131
00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:27.439
soispoke: it needs to somehow become a gas that's part of the verify frame gas you would use.

132
00:18:27.970 --> 00:18:35.050
soispoke: And, you know, if you have, like, 16 routes, it actually ends up costing quite a lot, like, 40,000 gas.

133
00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:39.339
soispoke: So that's one reason we would want to sort of, like, keep it in the envelope.

134
00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:41.679
lightclient: Why would you have 16 roots?

135
00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:52.080
soispoke: Like, the EIP defines this as, like, max, you know, max 16 routes, per SLAD.

136
00:18:54.450 --> 00:19:04.239
soispoke: Like, if you have, like, 16 different privacy dApps that want to update, like, their routes, then you could have 16 routes.

137
00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:13.410
soispoke: And in that case, that will actually become, you know, quite expensive.

138
00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:22.139
soispoke: to have this, as part of the verify gas, and we… we do want to sort of, yeah, make it as low as possible so we can

139
00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:27.259
soispoke: allow private transactions in the public webpool. So there's a bit of a tension there.

140
00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:34.719
lightclient: The privacy pools update their recent route in the verify param?

141
00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:43.570
soispoke: I mean, if you don't… if it's not part of the envelope, I think it…

142
00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:49.799
soispoke: That gas cost has to be taken into account in the… In… in the verified.

143
00:19:49.800 --> 00:20:02.180
lightclient: I just thought that… well, I thought that the privacy protocols themselves, whenever you would use the privacy protocol, it would update… it would call the recent routes contract and send an update.

144
00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:06.829
lightclient: Whereas in the verify frame, all you're doing is checking that this, like.

145
00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:11.279
lightclient: piece of call data matches a root that's in the ring buffer.

146
00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:18.380
soispoke: Right.

147
00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:21.879
lightclient: It can't write to storage in the verify frame, typically.

148
00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:35.519
soispoke: Yeah, I need to think a bit more about this, but or, like, double-check, and we can maybe take it offline, but I think last time I… last time I…

149
00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:37.620
soispoke: looked,

150
00:20:37.910 --> 00:20:46.259
soispoke: the sort of, like, frame solution for recent routes instead of in the envelope, like, costs a bit more, I guess, in the very first frame. I can just, like, double…

151
00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:47.630
lightclient: Yeah. Okay.

152
00:20:49.430 --> 00:21:06.480
Toni Wahrstätter: Yeah, and just to add, if it becomes a problem that we waste too much gas in the verify frame for recent root-related tasks, then we can also just exempt it from counting against this limit, because the limit is anyway only for the mempool.

153
00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:14.229
Toni Wahrstätter: So, we could just say, okay, it's very deterministic what is going to happen, and we just extend it.

154
00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:17.469
soispoke: Yeah, that's fair.

155
00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:31.969
soispoke: Maybe another question related to this, and I don't want to take the whole call, but, like, should it still be, like, an EIP, though? Like, I mean, the sort of, like, where we want to have these recent routes

156
00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:42.059
soispoke: changes, if we wanted to have, like, a… if we don't want it to have it in the transaction envelope, but I think, like, it would be still nice to define it and…

157
00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:46.460
soispoke: And have a spec for it,

158
00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:51.519
soispoke: So is that still sort of, like, the common shared sentiment, or not?

159
00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:56.969
lightclient: I think an EIP makes sense.

160
00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:06.079
lightclient: As long as it's, you know, clear that it's… if we do go down this path of not updating the envelope, it's just clear that this is,

161
00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:12.100
lightclient: A rule for the public transaction pool, and not the change of the protocol.

162
00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:14.210
soispoke: Okay. Yeah, that makes sense.

163
00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:22.770
