00:43:15.050 --> 00:43:17.420
lightclient: Why do you want things to be so contract-based?

337
00:43:19.610 --> 00:43:38.699
Chris - Base: Ultimately, I think that's, like, a way that won't need… like, you know, the address space is infinite, and we can always, well, not infinite, obviously, infinite for, you know, our purposes, but, like, we can always add more features that way over, kind of, envelope changes, I think.

338
00:43:40.830 --> 00:43:59.269
lightclient: Sure, but I think that there's, like, other… I mean, I feel like you also mentioned in cases around, like, the TX context precompile that you have with AD130, saying, like, okay, it's nice that this is a contract, but, like, we have the ability to make these native features opcodes, and opcodes are…

339
00:43:59.470 --> 00:44:03.969
lightclient: More easy to reason about and use as a contract.

340
00:44:04.860 --> 00:44:21.730
lightclient: I don't really understand, like, why you place this, like, premium on, you know, the contract in that sense. Like, there are definitely, like, nice times to use it, but I think that there's also, like, generally a lot of good times to use opcodes, and we have the ability to do that.

341
00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:28.630
Chris - Base: Yeah, I think that's a fair take. I think… I've said before, like, you know.

342
00:44:30.280 --> 00:44:39.860
