369
00:47:58.240 --> 00:48:06.470
marc | wolovim: There was one more question from Matt. I don't know if you want to raise it here. How do clients feel about removing the signature list and instead having a frame mode signature?

370
00:48:06.880 --> 00:48:19.719
lightclient: Yeah, this was one of the other things that I was wanting to raise at the end of the call, and Chris kind of also pointed at it in the diagrams, but something that I've been thinking a little bit about is

371
00:48:19.980 --> 00:48:24.050
lightclient: Do we need this signature list on the outer part of the transaction?

372
00:48:24.450 --> 00:48:35.040
lightclient: Originally, the rationale in my mind for having this on the outside of the transaction was I felt that signatures were a bit of a special…

373
00:48:35.210 --> 00:48:51.609
lightclient: primitive that kind of existed outside of the execution of the transaction. Like, the transaction really wasn't concerned with the, you know, actual verification of the signature. All that the transaction cares about is the result of those computations.

374
00:48:51.720 --> 00:49:05.889
lightclient: And also those signatures might be used in different places throughout the transaction. So it felt a little weird to require it to be included multiple times, you know, inside of a frame doing something else.

375
00:49:06.730 --> 00:49:28.080
lightclient: Also, there was a pretty strong push from Dano about having the signatures on the outside of the transaction object, so that it was easier to strip out the transaction data in the future when we do aggregation. So, for those reasons, we kind of went down this signature list on the outside of the transaction object.

376
00:49:28.120 --> 00:49:32.470
lightclient: I think that it's very possible to do it inside the transaction object.

377
00:49:32.510 --> 00:49:36.490
lightclient: And to treat things more like, addresses.

378
00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:42.629
lightclient: the main… the main issue I've run into, which I'm curious, like, what other people think about, is that…

379
00:49:42.830 --> 00:49:47.859
lightclient: Right now, one benefit of the signature list being on the outside is that for every transaction.

380
00:49:48.110 --> 00:49:54.330
lightclient: the ID which you specify the signature scheme you're using is just one byte.

381
00:49:54.720 --> 00:49:58.759
lightclient: Unless you're using an arbitrary signature, which would then be the address.

382
00:49:59.190 --> 00:50:04.600
lightclient: This ends up being, like, a pretty…

383
00:50:05.220 --> 00:50:10.129
lightclient: Big amount of data to have every single transaction include.

384
00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:13.669
lightclient: Sure, we can have precompiles which have tons of leading zeros.

385
00:50:14.030 --> 00:50:22.770
lightclient: And we won't put quite as much data over the wire, but at the end of the day, this is still a lot of RLP, this is a lot of JSON that has to be handled.

386
00:50:22.890 --> 00:50:26.300
lightclient: And it feels quite wasteful.

387
00:50:27.280 --> 00:50:38.260
lightclient: I wish that there was a better way to do this. I don't think it is a good idea for us to have a special address encoding scheme just for the signature frames. That feels…

388
00:50:38.910 --> 00:50:40.759
lightclient: Like, another wrong approach?

389
00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:46.320
lightclient: But I don't know if anyone has thought more about… This idea at all.

390
00:50:55.710 --> 00:51:00.069
lightclient: Luca is asking if it's similar to A288,

391
00:51:00.760 --> 00:51:03.360
